    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Triumph TF-E Bikes Get Even More Enduro With Updates for the 2027 Model Year\nIn Another Pointless PR Stunt, Geely's Lynk & Co Drives the 07 GT off a Cliff To Show How Safe It Is\n12 photos\nPhoto: @InsideChinaAuto via X\nYou know that car sales are bad in China when every carmaker resorts to crazy PR stunts to promote their vehicles. Geely's Lynk & Co may have gone too far in this quest by throwing its 07 GT station wagon off a cliff at 70 kph. Surprisingly, all doors can still open after the ordeal. However, it's unlikely that the fall would be survivable for anyone inside the cabin.\nIt's no secret that the Chinese car market has entered a tailspin, with October sales recording a 27% decline compared to 2025. Companies with a global footprint have tried their best to offload production to overseas markets. This explains why car exports have surged 60% in October. However, they could not offset the local sales slump, with the overall market still losing over 9% of combined sales (domestic and exports).\nDesperate times call for desperate measures, they say. In this case, it's PR-stunt time, with Chinese carmakers racing for the craziest and goofiest demonstrations. Just yesterday, we wrote about BYD resorting to some interesting acrobatics to promote the Denza BAO 5. Specifically, the SUV proved it could tow a Boeing 747 even without two wheels, which is probably what most customers would want to do with their cars.\nNow, we have Geely trying to steal the show with another act meant to promote the Lynk & Co 07 GT. Towing capacity and power were good for the Denza, but for a family car, Geely chose safety. There's no official Euro NCAP rating yet for the station wagon that debuted in Paris last month. So, what better way of proving its safety can you find, other than throwing the car off a cliff?\nPhoto: @InsideChinaAuto via X And so the story went, with Lynk & Co designing a ramp to thrust the 07 GT into the air and down into the abyss. During the video shared on social media, the car rolls over several times on a steep mountainside, both longitudinally and laterally. If you've seen it in movies, you'd expect the car to start throwing body parts and probably explode by the time it hit the bottom, but it didn't.\nEven more impressively, all doors could still open, while the glass roof, although cracked, was still protecting the cabin. Again, exactly the scenario that every family wants to test before buying a new car. As impressive as the wreckage looked at the end of the demonstration, I seriously doubt any passenger would still be alive after so many rolls and bumps.\nChen Qu, Geely's Global Communications chief, thinks otherwise, as he reposted the video on X with the text \"we have the safest car in the world.\" He admits that Volvo might disagree, but it's OK, because Volvo is also owned by Geely, so it's a family quarrel. We have the safest car in the world, don't argue this with me, except you @volvocars — Kevin Chen (@Geely_Kevin) November 19, 2026\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nlynk co 07 gt Lynk Co car safety falling off a cliff\nAbout the author: Cristian Agatie\nAfter his childhood dream of becoming a \"tractor operator\" didn't pan out, Cristian turned to journalism, first in print and later moving to online media. His top interests are electric vehicles and new energy solutions. Full profile\nUP NEXT All 2026 Formula 1 Drivers Can Now Be Had as LEGO Minifigures for the First Time, Race Cars in Tow",
